# 3. DECISIONS THAT MUST BE PRESERVED

The following architectural and security decisions have already been made.

Do not change them without explaining the reason and obtaining my approval.

## Redis and rate limiting

- Redis is the production default.
- Production must not silently fall back to in-memory rate limiting.
- Redis is required in production when the production configuration specifies it.
- If required Redis infrastructure is unavailable, startup or request handling must fail according to the established failure policy.
- Local development may use explicitly configured memory storage.
- Degraded modes must be explicitly configured, logged, documented and global.
- No per-route override of the global rate-limit failure policy.

## AI quota

- Supabase is the production default quota store.
- Memory storage must be explicitly selected and must warn.
- Fail closed by default.
- Do not allow untracked or unlimited AI requests when production quota enforcement is unavailable.
- Quota is counted on attempt.
- There is no quota refund.
- Do not change the quota-counting semantics.

## Sensitive endpoints

These endpoints must always fail closed:

- `extract-plan`
- `ai-generate-physique`
- `ai-physique-analysis`

The physique routes are text-only, not image-bearing. `extract-plan` is the route that forwards files.

## Barcode endpoint

Barcode is rate-limited only, not quota-limited.

It follows the global rate-limit policy.

## Health endpoint

Keep the existing public health endpoint behaviour unchanged.

Do not expose internal Redis, quota, database or security configuration through the health endpoint.

## Supabase access

Supabase must remain read-only unless I explicitly authorise a specific operation.

Do not run migrations, modify database permissions, change dashboard settings or modify production configuration without my explicit approval.

